WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.100
FABIO YEON: All right.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.570
Let's get started, then.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.130
Welcome.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.900
This is IO-314.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.270
My name is Fabio Yeon.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.970
I'm one of the engineers on the
Global Container Engine Team

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.720
and tech lead on a team as well.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.610
"Google Container
Engine and the Path

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:21.586
to Cloud-Native Operations,"
or as I like to call it,

00:00:21.586 --> 00:00:24.600
"the pi talk."

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.631
Wow, no math person in the room.

00:00:26.631 --> 00:00:28.790
[CHUCKLES]

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:33.850
All right, so start
with the basics--

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:34.450
containers.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.270
How many people here have
used or are deploying

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.700
containers in production?

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.980
Wow, that's a
pretty good number.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.290
How many of you have played
around with containers,

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.150
in general?

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.276
All right, how many people
have heard about containers?

00:00:49.276 --> 00:00:51.750
There, OK.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.970
So let's start with,
then, some simple intro.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.410
What are containers?

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:02.200
The most succinct explanation
I've heard of a container

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.140
is that a virtual machine
virtualizes hardware.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.710
A container
virtualizes a kernel.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.290
So what happens, then, is if
you have a bunch of containers

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.310
on the same machine, they're
all sharing the same kernel.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.400
But the container runtime
provides enough abstraction

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.290
so each one of them
thinks they have

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.420
the machine for themselves.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.920
So, why are they useful?

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.310
Well, in this little
box called a container,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:34.030
you can put in just your
application, its dependencies,

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:35.680
and you're done.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.410
Compared to what you
had to do to set up

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.520
a deployment on a
VM or a bare metal,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.880
you don't have to install an OS.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.713
You don't have to
configure your networking.

00:01:45.713 --> 00:01:48.310
You don't have to configure
and set up the dependencies.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.700
It just shows you, your
application, your binaries,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.970
and a little container
image, and you're done.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.750
In many ways, this is what
I call getting serenity

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.258
in a sea of chaos.

00:02:01.258 --> 00:02:02.150
All right-- whoops.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.940
Clicker.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.482
So once you start playing
around with containers,

00:02:06.482 --> 00:02:08.440
you start running into
little more complicated,

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.580
complex scenarios.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.840
And this is where Kubernetes
comes in and helps you out.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.540
Your little box-- if you
have one or two of them,

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.500
sure, it's easy to get them
up and running and deployed.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.340
Once you start
getting more of those,

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.060
then you start getting
a little bit more help.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.090
Kubernetes helps you
ensure that there's

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.320
a place for your
container to run,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.680
makes sure that the proper
number of them are running,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.120
makes sure that the
network packets intended

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.760
for your containers gets routed
properly through the cluster

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.540
and arrives at the
appropriate place.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.810
And, of course, if
you use Kubernetes,

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.930
Google Container
Engine provides to you

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:55.390
the best and purest Kubernetes
experience you can get.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.370
Beyond just allowing to set up
a Kubernetes cluster easily,

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.800
Google Container Engine
strives to provide to you

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.350
management capabilities
that makes it easy

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.050
for you to maintain and
keep your Kubernetes cluster

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.380
running.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.550
So the short little
intro, let's dive

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.260
in a little bit
into what I'm going

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.980
to call cloud-native
management capabilities

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.590
and show you some of the things
that we do in Google Container

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.320
Engine to help you
be more productive

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.650
and to care less about the
machinations of your clusters

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.140
and go back to what you
want to do more, which

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.260
is maintain your applications
and your services

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.410
on Kubernetes.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.900
"Change is the only
constant in life."

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.600
I like this quote for
many different reasons.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.300
But that's because in
the little container box,

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.900
you have been able to
provide a level of stability

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.500
on your thing, on your
applications and your service.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.900
Remember that around
you, the clusters,

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.757
things may be
changing continually,

00:03:55.757 --> 00:03:57.340
and you have to have
a little strategy

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.910
to be able to manage
some of those changes.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.370
So just a quick
overview of the agenda.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.890
Gonna talk a little bit about
Kubernetes, the Kubernetes

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:08.910
versions and why the
version management

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.130
is kind of important for you.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.040
Talk a little bit
about node health--

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.790
what it takes to
maintain your node health

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.829
and how to evaluate that.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:19.110
There's a little bit about
operations logs and then

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:20.760
talk about some
strategies that we

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:28.890
have come up with for successful
operations of a cluster.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.380
So keeping up with Kubernetes.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.360
So remember, Kubernetes
is an open source project.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.730
As such, it has
its own ecosystem,

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.640
it has its own
community, and it also

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.910
has its own release cadence.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.710
Kubernetes strives to
get one minor version

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.030
release a quarter.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:55.216
Now [CHUCKLES] this is where
a minor version is like a 1.4,

00:04:55.216 --> 00:04:58.520
a 1.5, or sometime
later this month,

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.310
I think a 1.6 is
slated to come out.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.590
It seems-- seemingly
like a small thing.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.910
So it's a small number change.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:10.510
But because Kubernetes is still
a fast-moving, fast-developing

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.100
platform, every
quarterly release

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.580
tends to be a fairly large
release, a fairly big release.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.330
New features gets launched,
some existing features

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:25.510
get upgraded from alpha to
beta, beta to ga, and so forth.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.500
So every minor
release of Kubernetes

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.830
comes in as a big package.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.750
If you look at the
release notes or change

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:36.570
logs for every minor release,
it is very comprehensive.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.020
It is very extensive.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.220
Beyond that, every couple of
weeks patch releases come out.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:47.840
These may be bug fixes, security
fixes, small improvements

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.180
that were missed, and so on.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.300
In Google Container
Engine, our goal

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.960
is to provide to you the
latest version of Kubernetes

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.000
as quickly as possible.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.750
Order days, if possible,
but if not, sooner.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:07.130
And we also provide with you
a collection of versions,

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.800
because we understand that
not everything is perfect.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.250
And therefore, sometimes you
want to hold back a little bit.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.190
So we provide to you the
latest version of Kubernetes--

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.660
one older version,
plus the latest version

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.340
of every previous minor
version that's been released.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.480
Now, I know that's a
little hard to parse, which

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.060
is why I put an example here.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:33.140
Currently, the latest version
of Kubernetes available is 1.53,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:34.190
and that is available.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.250
The previous version
of that is 1.52,

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.957
which is the previous
patch release of 1.5.

00:06:39.957 --> 00:06:42.290
And then we try to provide
the latest version of the 1.4

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:46.280
branch, which is 1.49, and the
latest version from the 1.3

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.280
branch, which is 1.310.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.340
So why does collection
of versions,

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:56.735
why this seemingly subset of
minor versions and not others?

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.500
In Kubernetes, the
master and the nodes

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.570
can be running
different versions

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.000
of Kubernetes binaries.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.490
And the official
support states that--

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:16.320
by the community and so forth,
is that the master and the node

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.380
can differ by, at most,
two minor versions.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.670
So Kubernetes Master
is running at 1.5.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:26.820
The oldest version of Kubernetes
that can be running on a node

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.010
and still be
considered supported

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.470
is something in the 1.3--

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.950
1.4, 1.2, 1.5, 1.3.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:38.040
When 1.6 comes out,
that will go up to 1.4.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.070
Anything beyond that, meaning
that any version skewed

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.460
beyond those two minor releases
is considered out of support

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:48.540
by both Kubernetes and
Google Container Engine.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:51.120
It goes into what is
called best effort.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.850
Your node may still be
running, it may still work.

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:58.830
But the likelihood is that some
functionality may or may not

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:03.950
work and may break
in the future.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.650
So Container Engine,
remember, will automatically

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.900
upgrade your cluster masters.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.187
This is one of the things
that we offer to you

00:08:12.187 --> 00:08:14.520
when you create a Kubernetes
cluster on Google Container

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:15.020
Engine.

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:19.020
Among the things that
we do in managing

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.430
a master is to upgrade it to
ensure that it is at the latest

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.380
version of Kubernetes.

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:30.510
So, as an example, if six months
ago you created a Kubernetes

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:36.059
cluster on Container Engine
at version 1.3, at some point,

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:39.109
your master was upgraded to 1.4.

00:08:39.109 --> 00:08:41.400
And if you didn't do anything
[INAUDIBLE] nodes at 1.3,

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.270
you were still OK.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.880
It's one version difference.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.160
At some point in
the near past, it

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:48.930
was probably upgraded to 1.5.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:53.130
So now your master is at 1.5
and your nodes remained at 1.3.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.920
And 1.16 comes out,
and at some point

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.680
in the future, when Google
Container Engine upgrades

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.120
your masters to 1.6--

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.920
uh-oh.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.870
If you have upgraded
or nodes, you're

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:07.150
not officially out of support.

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:11.155
So if [INAUDIBLE] to
do anything, because

00:09:11.155 --> 00:09:13.530
of the management of the master
and the auto upgrade that

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.720
exists, you have to
upgrade your nodes

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.170
at least once every six to
nine months to at least stay

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.970
with the support band.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:25.080
Of course, we highly recommend
that you upgrade more often

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.420
to pick up some bug
fixes and security

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:32.240
fixes that may have [INAUDIBLE]
show up in Kubernetes.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.966
So we come now to the
very first feature

00:09:34.966 --> 00:09:36.590
that we have on Google
Container Engine

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.840
to kind of help you manage
some of this change.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:41.920
Node auto upgrade.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:47.090
Just like the cluster master,
where we manage it for you,

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:50.200
node auto upgrade
will monitor the nodes

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:51.950
that you have in the clusters.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:54.920
And if we detect it, it
is, shall we say, older

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.490
than the master, we will
automatically trigger

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.010
an upgrade of the nodes.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:03.130
It is the same exact logic
that is applied if you

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.680
upgrade your nodes manually.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.040
It can be enabled and
disabled at any time.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:15.060
And just briefly, you
can actually do this--

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:17.820
whether the auto upgrade
is enabled or not,

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.645
you can always upgrade
your nodes manually first.

00:10:20.645 --> 00:10:24.035
If you want to try
out, test out things,

00:10:24.035 --> 00:10:27.550
or try to get the latest version
into your cluster sooner.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:30.060
Now, we kind of try to do this
also in a very good judicious

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.420
manner.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:36.920
Patch upgrades, because they're
typically bug fixes or security

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.900
fixes, tend to be applied
much more quickly.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:43.820
So if my nodes are at
1.50 or 1.51, for example,

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.820
and I enable this feature, I
will probably be upgraded to 1.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:50.550
153 fairly quickly.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.520
Because we understand the minor
version, changes on Kubernetes

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.110
can be more disruptive.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.360
And even though Kubernetes
and the community

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.630
tries very hard to make sure
that it is not it is not

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.500
very disruptive and
lack of compatibility

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.350
and all those things
are taken care of--

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.570
but because we understand
that sometimes they

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.460
can be a little bit
more disruptive,

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:12.120
minor version updates happens
at a slightly slower pace.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.970
So, for example, the
1.4 to 1.5 upgrades

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:22.440
will happen either later cadence
than the minor patch releases.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.422
So how to enable this thing?

00:11:24.422 --> 00:11:25.630
You can enable in the gcloud.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:28.740
When you create a cluster, it
is on the gcloud beta channel

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.100
set of commands.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.430
The flag is a enabled
dash auto-upgrade.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.920
You can also do it when
you create a node pool,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:40.730
and you can also do it in the
UI at cluster creation time.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:43.910
Like I mention, you can
also enable or disable

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:48.200
disk of ability on node
pool at any given time.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:54.150
One thing to keep in mind,
if you disable this feature

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:57.500
while an auto
upgrade is running,

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:58.980
it will run to completion.

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:02.010
It just means that next time
you do a scan of your clusters,

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:05.730
it will not auto upgrade it.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.010
Now, let's pick a little
bit behind the scenes

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.770
as to what Google Container
Engine is doing for you when

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:15.360
this thing happens and go over
the set of scenarios in case

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.750
you want to opt out of
this and do it yourself.

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.170
So first thing, as I mentioned
before, you can always

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.540
trigger upgrades manually--
does not affect the settings

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:27.675
for auto upgrades.

00:12:27.675 --> 00:12:31.410
The first thing is that every
week, Google Container Engine

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:35.490
does a push, and we
do release nodes.

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:37.620
Among the things we publish
in this released node,

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:41.610
along with bug fixes and
teacher announcements,

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.950
is what versions of
Kubernetes, if any,

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:48.240
are made available
to you as a user.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.330
We also provide the same
functionality or notification,

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:54.540
both in the CLI, via gcloud
whenever you list it,

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:57.110
or in the UI.

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:59.400
Notice that there
are two versions that

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.380
are listed in this
particular output

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.280
have two different
types of asterisks.

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:07.740
The more asterisks there are,
typically the worse, the more

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.607
dangerous, the worse it is.

00:13:09.607 --> 00:13:11.940
In this case, it is telling
you that while my cluster is

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:13.890
one version behind
and the second one is

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.740
two versions behind,
I, unfortunately,

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.840
did not have one that was
three versions behind.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:21.430
But you can imagine
that would be--

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:24.210
the mourning would be even
more severe in this one.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.010
And in the UI, of
course, I think

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.170
there the upgrade
available notification

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:30.659
is available, as well,
whenever an upgrade is

00:13:30.659 --> 00:13:31.700
available for your nodes.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.130
If you want something a
little more programmatic,

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:40.290
gcloud container get
server config will give you

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.380
all the current
information about

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:46.890
the potential configurations
of your Google Container Engine

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:48.710
for a particular zone.

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:50.220
And its particular
output, you can

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.780
see that the default
cluster version is 1.53,

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:56.770
but the valid masters
are now 1.53 and 1.49,

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.040
which is the latest version
from 1.4, which we still

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.530
are currently
supporting at this time.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:04.560
And then your nodes can be
any number of these versions.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:09.920
Notice that even though 1.27
is still available, it is only

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:13.680
supported if you actually have
your master at the 1.4 branch.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.980
If you have a master in
the one 1.5 of Kubernetes,

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.050
the oldest version
of Kubernetes that

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:22.070
could be supported on the
node is in the 1.3 branch.

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:28.930
So thinking a little bit back.

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.150
I mentioned that Google
Container Engine managers

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:32.030
your masters.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:35.560
One of the things that
we do is upgrade them.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:39.370
You yourself can trigger an
early upgrade of the master

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:43.120
before we get to it, if
you still choose to do it.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.270
And the option is
from the command line.

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:48.520
It's a Container Engine
upgrade, and then you

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:50.889
add the dash, dash
master option.

00:14:50.889 --> 00:14:53.305
This tells the system that you
want to upgrade the master.

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:00.600
And then you can go ahead
and trigger a manual upgrade

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.070
of your nodes with a send
command, except this time,

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:05.500
you just take out the
dash, dash master.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:09.235
Why do we upgrade
the master first?

00:15:09.235 --> 00:15:12.750
It's because the
node can, at most,

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:15.390
be at the same
version as the master.

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:18.240
You cannot have a node
that is a version higher,

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:19.870
even a patch version.

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:24.390
So in the hypothetical world,
the 1.54 were to come out today

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:30.030
and made available to
Container Engine customers.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:34.150
If you want to try out 1.54
before we upgrade your cluster,

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:37.080
you would have to upgrade
your masters first to 1.54

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.790
and then upgrade your
nodes in your node pools

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:41.274
to 1.54 manually.

00:15:41.274 --> 00:15:42.690
And this is
regardless whether you

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.724
have enabled auto upgrade
for your nodes or not.

00:15:45.724 --> 00:15:46.890
You can just do it yourself.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.890
Ah, so we've started
doing an upgrade

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.860
or we trigger an
upgrade of the nodes,

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:56.470
and you detect that
something is going awry,

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:59.492
something has gone wrong,
and you need to stop.

00:15:59.492 --> 00:16:01.450
We have two sets of
commands that are currently

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:04.240
in the alpha channel of gcloud.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:07.304
The first one-- actually,
the second one this list,

00:16:07.304 --> 00:16:09.220
but I think the more
important one is you just

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:11.350
want the upgrade to stop.

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:13.960
So you call gcloud, alpha
container operations,

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:16.210
cancel and give you
the operation ID, which

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:19.420
you can get it from listing the
operations currently running.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:20.920
The operation list
is probably going

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:23.590
to say something
like auto upgrade

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:25.340
or a manual upgrade,
cluster upgrade.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:27.700
Node upgrade, I think.

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:29.740
You can cancel the operation.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:33.700
Cancelling an
operation does not stop

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:36.380
an ongoing upgrade of a node.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:40.990
So if a cluster has five
nodes and you detected that

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:42.910
after the first one,
something has gone south

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:43.784
and you want to stop.

00:16:43.784 --> 00:16:47.640
If the second node upgrade
has already started,

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:51.390
the cancelling of
the operation will--

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:55.780
that upgrade will finish,
but the subsequent upgrades

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:59.140
of the nodes in the node
pool will not start.

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.526
The upgrade will be
canceled at that point.

00:17:01.526 --> 00:17:03.400
Now, your cluster is
going to be in this kind

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:05.829
of a mixed-mode
version between couple

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:08.710
of nodes are in the new
version, a couple of nodes

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:10.780
in the old version.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:12.730
So then you can do
what's called a rollback.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:16.900
If you call gcloud alpha
container node pools rollback,

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:21.099
we will then take those nodes
that were at the newer version

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:24.430
and roll them back to
the previous version.

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:27.865
Couple things to keep in mind,
as well, as you're doing this.

00:17:27.865 --> 00:17:30.340
This is a recreation of a VM.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:33.250
So if you happen to have
any local configuration

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:36.100
or local data
stored on those VMs,

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:39.369
fortunately, they don't not
survive an upgrade operation

00:17:39.369 --> 00:17:39.910
at this time.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:45.410
Healthy nodes, healthy clusters.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:52.020
Keeping your nodes healthy
can be a little tricky.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:56.542
The nodes can go unhealthy
for a number of reasons.

00:17:56.542 --> 00:17:57.750
You can run out of resources.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:17:59.750
Maybe you over provisioned
stuff and you used

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:02.170
too much memory, too much CPU.

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:03.860
It can run on a local disk.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:07.080
Maybe you have a configuration
problem or a bug in Kubelet

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:08.930
that is causing it to crash.

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:11.185
Maybe your workload is
triggering a kernel bug,

00:18:11.185 --> 00:18:13.310
or, of course, network
segmentation network drivers

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:16.550
crash, so on and on.

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:20.360
Some of these are auto
recoverable by Kubernetes,

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:22.460
specifically the first
two, where you run out

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:24.570
of memory or resource.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.590
Some signals provided
to Kubernetes

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:30.500
to the cluster master and
the rescheduler controller

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:32.720
on the master, you're
supposed to observe

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:35.900
these things happening and say,
oh, this node is overloaded.

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:38.120
Let me just take away
some pods from it

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:39.730
and scatter them elsewhere.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:41.480
So they're self-healing
for the most part.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:44.080
As long as you have enough
resources in your clusters,

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:46.860
then there's nothing
you need to do.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.790
But if your click Kubelet
starts crashing on you,

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:51.950
if your current node
became deadlocked

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:55.730
because you happened to trigger
a kernel bug, what then, then?

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:58.640
This is where it starts getting
a little more interesting.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.610
Second thing to remember
about node health

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:05.300
is that it is the master's
evaluation of your node's

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:07.860
health that is important.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:08.750
Why?

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.830
Because it is the cluster
master that determine what pods

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:13.700
are scheduled where.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.420
So if your master thinks that
your node is unhealthy or vice

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:20.780
versa, you may not
schedule anything on it

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.060
if it's considered-- if
it thinks it's unhealthy,

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:26.530
or if it's giving a false signal
that is healthy but it is not,

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:30.391
and we try to schedule work
into an unhealthy node.

00:19:30.391 --> 00:19:31.890
So that's something
to keep in mind.

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.459
It's kind of important, is
that the self-evaluation

00:19:35.459 --> 00:19:37.500
of the health of the node
is less important here.

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:40.290
It is what the master
thinks of your node's health

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:42.570
at that time that is important.

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.150
And, of course,
repairs like upgrades

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:49.830
are typically limited to
recreations of a node.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:50.790
Oops, wrong button.

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:56.500
So this comes to a second
[INAUDIBLE] container

00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:01.150
on Google Container Engine to
help you manage your cluster.

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.550
It is node auto-repair.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:07.480
It is the semantic equivalent
of the master repair

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:10.120
that we already do for
your cluster today.

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:13.480
But this-- this is where we
are observing and monitoring

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:14.800
your nodes.

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:17.470
We are taking into
account the master's view

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.240
of your node health state.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:22.120
We are also evaluating
other signals around it.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.490
We take a look at the signals
from the managed instance

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:26.320
group backing your node pool.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:28.240
We also sometimes take
a look at the signals

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:31.180
provided by the node itself.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:35.770
With those signals, the of it
all, be coming to an evaluation

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:38.460
whether your node
is healthy or not.

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:41.830
Too many unhealthy signals
will trigger repair--

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:44.080
in this case, a
recreation of the node, in

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.420
attempt to get it
back into a good state

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.830
and having rejoined the cluster.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:51.430
We also try to make sure that
we rate limit this so that we're

00:20:51.430 --> 00:20:53.920
not doing this too often.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.530
Now, why rate limit?

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:59.730
It's because sometimes
the node going on health

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:02.590
and may be triggered
by your workload.

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:04.885
And if you start triggering
this wait too often,

00:21:04.885 --> 00:21:06.760
we may be in a situation
where the node never

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:10.460
is alive long enough
to do any useful work.

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:13.840
So we have somebody do some
of the rate limiting as well.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:16.130
Currently in beta--
as a matter of fact,

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.510
I think it's rolling
out as we speak,

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:20.980
and so fairly soon you
should be able to have

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:26.510
this capability show up in
the UI and in the CLI as well.

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:27.660
How do I enable it?

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:30.160
Just like auto-repair,
the flag is

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:31.992
dash dash enable auto-repair.

00:21:31.992 --> 00:21:33.450
You can do this at
cluster creation

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.534
time, at node for creation time.

00:21:35.534 --> 00:21:37.200
And, as a matter of
fact, I would highly

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.930
recommend that you
enable both of them--

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:43.110
auto-repair and auto-repair, to
make your life a little easier

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.668
when you run Kubernetes on
a Google Container Engine.

00:21:48.972 --> 00:21:50.930
One more thing before I
go into the next slide,

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.750
which I've [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:55.360
You can also enable and
disable this at any given time.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.310
It is just like
for auto upgrades.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:22:00.910
You do a gcloud beta
container node pools update,

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:04.462
and you can enable or disable
auto-repair on your cluster--

00:22:04.462 --> 00:22:05.920
on your-- sorry--
on your node pool

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:08.570
at anytime you that you want.

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:11.730
So let's now peek
into behind the scenes

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.880
and see a little bit of
what Google Container

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:15.646
Engine is doing on your behalf.

00:22:15.646 --> 00:22:18.270
And kind of give you an idea if
you wanted to do this yourself,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.080
what kind of things
you have to do.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:26.210
So I mentioned that it
is the master's view

00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:28.070
of your node health
that matters.

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:30.590
So how does a master know
what's going on in a node?

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:34.970
The Kubelet publishes to
the master periodically

00:22:34.970 --> 00:22:36.360
a bunch of information.

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:38.150
And one of them
is its evaluation

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:41.990
of its node health plus what's
going on in its internals.

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:44.600
All this information is
available from the node object

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:46.400
from your Kubernetes master.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:48.440
So from the command
line, you yourself

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:51.782
can get the same data by calling
[INAUDIBLE] control, get nodes.

00:22:51.782 --> 00:22:53.240
Of course, in this
output, it gives

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:55.141
a little more
formatted-shortened version

00:22:55.141 --> 00:22:55.640
of it.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:58.550
But if you see the
JSAR or YAML output,

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:00.695
you can actually
get at the raw data.

00:23:00.695 --> 00:23:02.070
And in a little
bit, in the demo,

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:05.570
kind of show you how
I can look for this.

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:09.080
So you're listening your
nodes, and you're determined

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:13.435
and one of them has gone
unhealthy, not ready.

00:23:13.435 --> 00:23:14.810
So what are the
steps now for you

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:16.910
to try to get back
to a good state?

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:20.729
Well, the first thing you should
do is cordon off your nodes.

00:23:20.729 --> 00:23:22.520
What this thing does
is tell the scheduler,

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:25.550
hey, regardless of what's going
on in this particular node,

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:28.850
don't schedule any
further work on it.

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:32.330
Next thing we do, we
attempt to do a drain,

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:34.419
try to do a graceful
drain of all the pods,

00:23:34.419 --> 00:23:35.960
make sure they all
go away some place

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:38.570
so that the pod becomes empty.

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:40.760
This may or may not
succeed depending

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:44.630
upon the state of Kubelet and
other things on your node.

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:49.310
If your node had a hard deadlock
because of a kernel issue,

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:50.710
this may not work.

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:53.180
But at least we try.

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:57.245
The next thing is we try to list
your manage instance group back

00:23:57.245 --> 00:23:59.270
in your node pool.

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:01.160
And from that manage
instance group,

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:04.550
we have to identify the
specific node from that

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:07.140
and then try to tell the manage
instance group, hey, please

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:09.710
recreate that node.

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:10.940
Because we have set up--

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:13.190
on Google Container Engine
we have set up your cluster

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:16.730
using instance group
templates, a simple recreation

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:18.410
of it using the
existing template

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:21.450
gets your back a node
into your cluster.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:29.717
Operations log--
[CHUCKLING] as we

00:24:29.717 --> 00:24:31.800
start getting more and
more capabilities on Google

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:36.010
Container Engine to help
you manage your cluster more

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:40.310
easily, a question that keeps
coming up is, like, hey,

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:42.870
how do I find out when you
guys have done something

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:44.604
on my cluster?

00:24:44.604 --> 00:24:46.270
So the first that we
have done right now

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:50.050
is that we are augmenting the
operations log for a Container

00:24:50.050 --> 00:24:53.770
Engine to contain more
information about when

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:58.190
we do automated operations on
your clusters on your behalf.

00:24:58.190 --> 00:25:03.070
So before we had repair clusters
and upgrade masters available,

00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:06.220
and we're adding auto-repair
nodes and auto-upgrade nodes

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:09.730
as additional
operation log types

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:12.700
so that you can find out
whenever we have done

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:14.200
these things on your behalf.

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:19.320
How do you get to
the operational log?

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:21.360
Well, gcloud container
operations list

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:25.169
will give you a order list
of all the operations that

00:25:25.169 --> 00:25:26.460
have been done on your cluster.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:27.876
Actually, I think
it's beyond just

00:25:27.876 --> 00:25:31.980
your cluster-- is in your
project across your clusters.

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:34.300
And then if you do a
container operations describe

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:36.240
on an operation, it gives
a little more detail

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:40.410
as to what we did and
what the status was.

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:41.510
Were we successful?

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:42.645
Did we fail?

00:25:42.645 --> 00:25:43.240
So on and on.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:50.170
Node pools and
resource management--

00:25:50.170 --> 00:25:52.890
another favorite topic of mine.

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:55.320
How many people here have
heard about node pools

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.580
or have used node pools?

00:25:57.580 --> 00:25:58.570
Handful.

00:25:58.570 --> 00:25:59.850
OK, good.

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:02.130
So what are node pulls?

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:05.460
Node pulls-- the shortest
definition is just a collection

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.950
of nodes managed together.

00:26:07.950 --> 00:26:11.490
From a Google Container
Engine perspective,

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:15.540
what it means is that you
can have multiple node

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:18.390
pools in your cluster,
each are defining

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:20.540
a particular configuration.

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:23.910
And When I say configuration,
I mean machine type,

00:26:23.910 --> 00:26:27.330
machine resources, and
Kubernetes versions,

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:30.330
scopes, and on and on.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:31.830
Within a node pool,
all the machines

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:33.660
have to have the same
exact configuration.

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:35.493
They have to be the
same exact machine type,

00:26:35.493 --> 00:26:37.295
they have to have the
same exact machine--

00:26:37.295 --> 00:26:38.570
Kubernetes version.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:41.360
They must have the same
exact machine resources.

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:45.570
So if I ask for an
n1-standard-4, with, let's say,

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:50.220
two local SSDs, every single
machine in that node pool

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:52.950
will have the same
configuration.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:56.010
But, of course, I can have
multiple of these in a cluster,

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:58.680
so this gives me
an additional level

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:01.727
of functionality--
flexibility, as well, where

00:27:01.727 --> 00:27:03.810
I can have different machine
types and different--

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:07.890
and mix and match these
to meet my workload

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:11.380
and my resource
needs more carefully.

00:27:11.380 --> 00:27:14.350
And this is also a foundational
unit for many management stuff

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:16.800
that I talked about, so
when, before, I was talking

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:19.590
about cluster
creation, enabling it

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:22.380
at cluster creation
or node pool creation,

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:26.772
when you create a cluster on
Google Container Engine, what

00:27:26.772 --> 00:27:28.230
is actually happening
is that we're

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:29.730
creating the master
and so forth,

00:27:29.730 --> 00:27:31.830
and we are also
creating a default node

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:33.462
pool for your cluster.

00:27:33.462 --> 00:27:35.670
So all the options available
for creating a node pool

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:40.140
are available on your
cluster creation as well.

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:42.079
So let's take a
look and some things

00:27:42.079 --> 00:27:44.620
that you can do with node pools
might be kind of interesting.

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:48.402
So can we switch to the
demo machine, please?

00:27:48.402 --> 00:27:51.890
Ah, OK.

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:57.690
So you go container
clusters list.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:02.610
And here I have my cluster
that I created for this demo.

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:06.570
It is currently running on
