00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.190
A custom 2x2048,
which signals to me

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:59.040
that this is a two-core machine
with only two gigs of RAM.

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:05.560
Let's then describe
the main pool--

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:09.420
oops, let me pipe that.

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:11.380
OK.

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:15.040
n1 [INAUDIBLE] 4,
identification scope's defined.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:16.944
The number of initial
counts enabled.

00:29:16.944 --> 00:29:18.360
And, of course,
in our management,

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:21.280
I can see that I have enabled
both auto repair and auto

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:23.820
upgrade for this
particular pool.

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:28.740
OK, I think--
interesting on this one.

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:32.640
But if I go to my pre-pool,
among the things that you'll

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:34.020
notice on this
particular listing

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:39.210
is that this thing is
comprised of preemptible VMs.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:43.140
Preemptible VMs, of course,
are options provided to you

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:48.060
from DCP, where the costs
for these are lower.

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:52.110
But in return, the VM may be
preempted underneath of you

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.610
with a moment's notice.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:30:00.890
But if you have a workload that
are preemptible-friendly or can

00:30:00.890 --> 00:30:04.590
be preemptible easily,
this is an easy way for you

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:07.920
to instantiate a couple node
pools in your cluster to have

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.740
this and leverage it--

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.805
the lower of cost
that they provide.

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:20.397
And then, of course, the final
one, which is my lowmeme pool.

00:30:20.397 --> 00:30:22.730
It is just like my main pool,
except the difference here

00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:27.200
is that I'm running
custom 2x2048 machines.

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:28.734
So, like I said,
very low memory,

00:30:28.734 --> 00:30:30.650
high, superior kind of
workloads would benefit

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:35.610
from this kind of node pool.

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:40.090
So previously, I have already
pre-deployed a number of pods

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:41.575
on this particular cluster.

00:30:41.575 --> 00:30:44.050
So let's take a
look at all of them,

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:45.400
make sure they're all running.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:50.590
So I have deployments
that are very high CPU.

00:30:50.590 --> 00:30:53.609
Some deployments that are
just regular, main workloads.

00:30:53.609 --> 00:30:55.150
And I have something
that I'm calling

00:30:55.150 --> 00:30:57.100
preemptible workloads
are workloads

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.500
that are eminently preemptible.

00:30:59.500 --> 00:31:01.750
With a little bit of trickery--

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:04.270
and this is where I will
cut and paste the command,

00:31:04.270 --> 00:31:10.110
because I can never
remember from memory.

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:12.090
I called [INAUDIBLE]
control pause

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:14.380
provided a JsonPath
path that says,

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:20.750
hey, fish out this little fields
from your particular listing

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:23.810
and then combine them
to write this output.

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:26.720
So in this view, you can see
that all my pods are listed

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:29.300
there like before, but
now I'm also listing

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:31.700
which node they are running on.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:35.330
And you can see this thing
scattered all over the place.

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:38.750
My high-CPU workload's
running in a variety

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:39.560
different machines.

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:42.889
My main machines-- I have
main workloads running

00:31:42.889 --> 00:31:44.180
on a variety of other machines.

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.330
But more importantly--
they've been not a good idea--

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:53.520
I have some workloads that are
running on my preemptible VMs.

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:57.470
Should be OK, maybe,
but certainly not ideal.

00:31:57.470 --> 00:32:00.540
Because maybe
they're not workloads

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:03.540
that you want to run
on a preemptible VM.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:07.560
So, how do we then
take care of that?

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:12.844
Let's take a look,
then, in a node

00:32:12.844 --> 00:32:14.010
and see what I can find out.

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:19.000
So if I list the raw
Json object for a node,

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.480
you will notice a couple of
things here among the metadata

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:22.040
so far.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:25.770
It is that there's a
whole section for labels.

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:28.470
Some of these labels
are applied on each node

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:31.410
by the Kubernetes runtime,
and they are identified easily

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:36.700
by the kubernetes.io prefix.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:39.850
So thing like machine
architecture, in the 64,

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:40.570
machine OS--

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:42.490
Linux.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:45.910
Down here-- host name,
host name on machine.

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.350
You'll also notice
a few of these

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:50.260
that are actually set by
Google Container Engine itself.

00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:53.140
They are prefixed
with cloud.google.com.

00:32:53.140 --> 00:32:55.300
In this case by default,
every single node

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:57.580
that we create on
Google Container Engine

00:32:57.580 --> 00:32:59.830
has, automatically,
as part of it,

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:02.740
the node pool name
associated with it.

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:08.790
And, of course, the other thing
there is this other label.

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:12.370
On Google Container Engine,
when you create a node pool,

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:15.610
you are allowed to provide
to it a comma-delimited list

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:21.240
of key value pairs that become
part of your node labels.

00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:27.270
So you can, then, use these on
your pods spec in Kubernetes

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:31.050
so that it ensure that
those workloads, those pods,

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:36.740
ends up at a node that
has this label in them.

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:38.820
One more thing I want
to show up along here.

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:43.780
This is another node.

00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:49.120
In this case, this happens to be
a VM that is a preemptible VM.

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:51.125
When you create a
preemptible VM--

00:33:51.125 --> 00:33:53.800
a node pool, sorry-- in
Google Container Engine,

00:33:53.800 --> 00:33:57.290
we will automatically add
apply this label as well,

00:33:57.290 --> 00:33:58.767
so you don't have to do it.

00:33:58.767 --> 00:34:00.850
You can certainly add more,
like I had done below,

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:04.090
where I've added the pre label
as well, where you can actually

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:05.260
add the Google Cloud Comp.

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:09.429
You can use this label as part
of a node selector on your pod

00:34:09.429 --> 00:34:12.139
spec as well.

00:34:12.139 --> 00:34:15.310
Oh, before I go off--

00:34:15.310 --> 00:34:18.620
I mentioned earlier
about node condition.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:26.790
If you want to explore
a little bit more

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:29.239
about doing the node
health evaluation yourself,

00:34:29.239 --> 00:34:33.000
every single node contains
this array of node conditions

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.630
under the conditions list.

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:37.424
And there are a
variety of these.

00:34:37.424 --> 00:34:40.710
Some of them talk
about [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.830
health, disc, memory, pressure.

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:46.440
And, of course, the bottom one
is whether the Kubelet is ready

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:48.020
or not.

00:34:48.020 --> 00:34:51.570
This is part of the signal
that is pushed from the node

00:34:51.570 --> 00:34:54.449
to the masters periodically
to let the master know

00:34:54.449 --> 00:34:56.219
what the status of the node is.

00:34:56.219 --> 00:34:59.220
And this is the signal that both
the master and Google Container

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:00.630
Engine uses--

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:02.070
well, one of the signals--

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:05.530
to determine whether the
node is healthy or not.

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:09.240
So back to this demo.

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:18.210
I have, then, deployment YAML
for my high-CPU workload.

