00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:49.580
I've used my custom labels that
I've set up on each node pool,

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:51.450
on this one, I
just decided to use

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:54.650
the one that Google
Container Engine sets

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:56.615
for preemptible VMs.

00:35:56.615 --> 00:35:58.490
Might be kind of nice,
because at this point,

00:35:58.490 --> 00:36:00.650
I'm not relying upon a
custom label that I've set

00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:03.350
or the pool name or
anything like that.

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:08.700
I'm just using an automatically
set label that Google Container

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:10.230
Engine has put on it.

00:36:10.230 --> 00:36:15.470
So it's available across
any VM that is printable.

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:20.629
Let me go ahead and replace
the deployment on Kubernetes.

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:29.662
And preempt.

00:36:29.662 --> 00:36:32.020
OK?

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:34.920
Give control, get pods.

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:36.980
Bunch terminating,
bunch running,

00:36:36.980 --> 00:36:39.130
bunch container running.

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:40.860
We'll let this run
for a little bit,

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:42.610
let everything kind
of settled down again.

00:36:48.668 --> 00:36:51.070
Mhm.

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:52.540
Wow, taking a little bit longer.

00:37:00.524 --> 00:37:04.430
OK, things seem to be
settling in, settling down.

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:05.990
Things are getting scheduled.

00:37:05.990 --> 00:37:06.930
Excellent.

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:09.200
Ah, everything is running again.

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:11.780
So if we go back to that
somewhat complicated Kubelet

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:16.080
control that I had before,
what you'll find here now

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:19.920
is all of my high
CPU deployment--

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:22.770
it is now running on
my low-memory pool.

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:24.210
My main workload
is my main pool.

00:37:24.210 --> 00:37:27.780
But more importantly than
anything else, my preemptible,

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:30.480
friendly workload
is now all scheduled

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:33.570
and running on preemptible VMs.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.090
So with this, I was
able to, shall we say,

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:39.170
make sure that my high-CPU
workloads end up on machines

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:41.460
that have very high CPU
of low amounts of memory--

00:37:41.460 --> 00:37:43.870
save me some money,
ensured that the proper VMs

00:37:43.870 --> 00:37:45.440
are assigned for those.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:47.040
My main workload
can be whatever.

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:51.090
My generic set of workloads
can go on the main workload.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:53.100
But more importantly,
my preemptible

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:57.480
workloads that low priority,
low cost are all set up

00:37:57.480 --> 00:38:00.660
and now are all running
on my preemptible VMs,

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:03.380
exactly as I desired to be.

00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:05.280
Couple more things
to keep in mind--

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:08.880
there are more node
selector options

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:13.440
becoming available to you as
part of the Kubernetes 1.6

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:14.130
release.

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:18.642
So, for example, node
selector right now is a match.

00:38:18.642 --> 00:38:20.100
One of the things
that is coming up

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:21.974
that might be kind of
interesting, especially

00:38:21.974 --> 00:38:26.970
in this kind of scenario,
is the [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:28.920
You may have workloads
that I don't really

00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:31.170
care where they run,
I just don't want them

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:33.810
to run on this type of VM--

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:35.254
preemptive VM, for example.

00:38:35.254 --> 00:38:37.420
So that's one of the options
that will come online--

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:38.880
available in the
1.6 timeframe, I

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:44.710
believe, where you can just
state that in your pod spec

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:48.830
and will be available
when you deploy them.

00:38:48.830 --> 00:38:52.900
So can you go back to
