00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:54.390
All right.

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:58.560
So a couple of slides I put it
in here for you to reference.

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:01.260
Feel free to take a look at it,
how to list some node pools,

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:04.330
how to create the node pools
that I've-- whoops, sorry--

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:06.580
that I've used for this demo.

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:08.540
Notice the machine
types, notice the dash,

00:39:08.540 --> 00:39:12.120
dash printable option as an
option, and the node labels,

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:15.660
how to specify these
on your node pools.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:18.380
And, of course, in assigning
pods to node pulls,

00:39:18.380 --> 00:39:23.566
it's the node selector
option in your YAML file.

00:39:23.566 --> 00:39:27.330
OK, some final notes.

00:39:27.330 --> 00:39:29.470
Upgrades can be disruptive.

00:39:29.470 --> 00:39:32.680
So to help mitigate
them, you can use--

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:35.610
the upgrades are for node pool.

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:38.400
So you kind of use
them to segment

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:42.870
the upgrades of your cluster
so that you can have either

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:47.170
a canary test kind of pool,
you can try out newer versions.

00:39:47.170 --> 00:39:50.352
So you have an existing workflow
that is running at version 1.51

00:39:50.352 --> 00:39:51.810
and you want to
try a 1.53, you can

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:54.450
spin up another
node pool at 1.53,

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:57.540
schedule some workload on
that particular node pool,

00:39:57.540 --> 00:39:59.760
make sure everything
works fine before you

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:02.730
let the rest of your
cluster be upgraded.

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:05.250
Consider having what I
call the one-node slack

00:40:05.250 --> 00:40:06.750
to ensure proper capacity.

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:10.170
When we do upgrades, we take
down one node, do the upgrade,

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:10.960
bring it back up.

00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:12.990
Go on to the next
node, and so on.

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:15.210
While the one node is
down and being upgraded,

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:18.370
it would be nice for you to have
enough slack on your cluster

00:40:18.370 --> 00:40:21.810
so that whatever pod was running
there can find a new home

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:24.570
while the upgrade is occurring.

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:26.250
Google Container
Engine limits you

00:40:26.250 --> 00:40:30.130
to having one cluster operation
running at any given time.

00:40:30.130 --> 00:40:34.290
Now just to differentiate
between the Container Engine

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:38.110
operation versus the Kubernetes
operation, for example,

00:40:38.110 --> 00:40:40.830
these are things like adding
or deleting a node pool,

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:43.830
updating a cluster-wide
configuration,

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:46.890
doing an upgrade, a
repair, any kind of events.

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:49.530
All those are serial on
Google Container Engine

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:52.260
for various logistic reasons.

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.410
So for the most part,
this restriction

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:57.510
is not a big deal
except if you're

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:01.627
upgrading a fairly large
cluster or node pool.

00:41:01.627 --> 00:41:03.210
And you can imagine
that because we're

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:05.370
doing the upgrade
sequentially, it

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:07.770
may take a little while
for it to complete.

00:41:07.770 --> 00:41:10.320
In the middle of it, if
you have to do something

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:14.200
on your cluster, go ahead
and cancel the upgrade.

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:16.225
It will stop at a good point.

00:41:16.225 --> 00:41:17.600
You can do whatever
operation you

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:19.890
have to do on your
clusters, and then

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:21.504
re-issue the upgrade command.

00:41:21.504 --> 00:41:23.670
It will just kind of pick
up from where it was left,

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:25.430
scanning your nodes
there we have,

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:28.290
skipping those that have
already been upgraded and just

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:31.260
keep on going the rest of them.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:32.940
And, of course,
we're truly trying

00:41:32.940 --> 00:41:35.504
to make progress and try to
minimize some disruptions.

00:41:35.504 --> 00:41:37.170
And we have more
capabilities that we're

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:40.920
going to be adding in a future
for upgrades and repairs.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:43.320
Some of the best practices
that we have found--

00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:46.440
pod disruptions are a reality.

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:48.744
Try to prepare your
workload for them.

00:41:48.744 --> 00:41:50.160
There are a number
of capabilities

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:53.100
being added to Kubernetes that
are gonna help you with that.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:54.900
So please do take
advantage of them.

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:58.020
And we'll keep integrating them
into Google Container Engine.

00:41:58.020 --> 00:42:00.640
Do use deployments of
replica sets for your pods.

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:03.142
Make sure that you have
enough sufficient redundancy.

00:42:03.142 --> 00:42:05.100
The second point is
actually kind of important.

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:07.740
In the cloud-native
world, please

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:13.020
to not write set once and
forget configuration scripts.

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:15.430
The mindset you need
to have is reconciler.

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:19.140
You need to write reconcilers
that read the current state.

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.540
If it's not the desired
state that you want,

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:24.120
do some action to
try to get there.

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:27.320
And then once it's completed,
go to sleep and then

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:29.790
start over from step 1 again.

00:42:29.790 --> 00:42:32.610
This way, whether it's a
repair event, an upgrade event,

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:34.530
or some other things
that happening outside

00:42:34.530 --> 00:42:37.000
of the container, if you
have some configuration

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:38.730
that you absolutely
need, you know

00:42:38.730 --> 00:42:42.120
that at some point, if it goes
out of alignment for whatever

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:44.520
reason, it is a temporary
thing that hopefully

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.580
should autocorrect itself.

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:49.570
And, of course,
please use node pools.

00:42:49.570 --> 00:42:51.910
Organize your resource more
efficiently in your cluster.

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:54.230
Allow you to have
[INAUDIBLE] configurations.

00:42:54.230 --> 00:42:56.460
Leverage different types of
machines, different types

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:59.190
of resources, and so on.

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:02.130
A quick recap--
change is constant.

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:03.970
Please be prepared.

00:43:03.970 --> 00:43:09.630
Do enable auto-upgrade,
and then try auto-repair.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:11.760
Operations logs--
please look into them

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:13.470
to see if-- whenever
we do something,

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:16.053
we'll try to make sure that they
all end up in your operations

00:43:16.053 --> 00:43:18.902
log for your clusters
so nothing is hidden.

00:43:18.902 --> 00:43:20.860
Use your node pools to
organize your resources.

00:43:20.860 --> 00:43:24.690
And, of course, think
reconciler, not set and forget.

00:43:27.372 --> 00:43:28.830
For those that may
not be familiar,

